RECNO Overflow Storage Design
=============================

This document describes RECNO's column-level overflow mechanism: how
large attribute values are stored off the main tuple, how the design
integrates with the surrounding RECNO subsystems (tuple deform, FSM,
WAL, VACUUM, MVCC visibility), how it differs from PostgreSQL's TOAST,
how it is expected to compare to TOAST in practice, and the prior art
that informed the design.  The intended audience is PostgreSQL
committers and contributors evaluating the RECNO table access method.

Source of record: src/backend/access/recno/recno_overflow.c plus the
structure and constant definitions in src/include/access/recno.h and
the WAL format in src/include/access/recno_xlog.h.


1. Problem Statement
====================

RECNO stores tuples in place on heap-style data pages.  A RECNO tuple
must fit within a single page:

    RECNO_MAX_TUPLE_SIZE =
        MAXALIGN_DOWN(BLCKSZ - RECNO_PAGE_OVERHEAD - sizeof(ItemIdData))

where RECNO_PAGE_OVERHEAD covers the standard page header plus the
RecnoPageOpaqueData special area.  For the default 8 KB page this is
roughly 8 KB minus a few dozen bytes.

Without an off-page mechanism, any row whose serialized form exceeds a
single page could not be stored at all, and even rows that merely
approach page size would pack poorly (one row per page, wasting space).
Overflow storage solves this for individual oversized column values.

The trigger is per-column, not per-row.  During tuple formation
(recno_tuple.c, RecnoFormTuple, "Phase 1b"), each variable-length,
non-external attribute whose size exceeds RECNO_OVERFLOW_THRESHOLD is
moved off-tuple:

    RECNO_OVERFLOW_THRESHOLD = RECNO_MAX_TUPLE_SIZE / 4

i.e. roughly 2 KB on an 8 KB page.  This threshold is deliberately
conservative: it keeps the main tuple small enough that many tuples
still share a page even when one or more columns are large, mirroring
TOAST's goal of keeping the main heap tuple compact.


2. On-Disk Structures
=====================

There are two structures and one host-tuple representation.

2.1 The inline overflow pointer (RecnoOverflowPtr)
--------------------------------------------------

When a column is overflowed, the value left behind in the main tuple is
itself a varlena whose payload is a RecnoOverflowPtr followed by an
optional inline prefix of the original bytes:

    [varlena header][RecnoOverflowPtr][inline_prefix_bytes...]

    typedef struct RecnoOverflowPtr {
        uint32       ov_magic;          /* "RVOP" sentinel */
        BlockNumber  ov_first_block;    /* first overflow record page */
        OffsetNumber ov_first_offset;   /* first overflow record offset */
        uint16       ov_padding;
        uint32       ov_total_length;   /* total reconstructed length */
        uint16       ov_inline_prefix;  /* prefix bytes stored after ptr */
        uint16       ov_flags;          /* reserved */
    } RecnoOverflowPtr;

The ov_magic sentinel (RECNO_OVERFLOW_PTR_MAGIC) is what lets the deform
path distinguish an overflow pointer from an ordinary varlena.
RecnoIsOverflowPtr() checks the size lower bound and the magic.  Because
this is a sentinel rather than a tagged varlena bit, the check requires
the value to be at least sizeof(RecnoOverflowPtr) and to carry the magic
in the first four payload bytes; ordinary user data of that size with
that exact leading word is astronomically unlikely but is, in principle,
the one fragility of the scheme (see Section 11, Open Questions).

The inline prefix is a configurable number of leading bytes of the
original value kept directly in the main tuple
(recno_overflow_inline_prefix GUC, default RECNO_OVERFLOW_DEFAULT_PREFIX
= 128).  Its purpose is to answer prefix-oriented predicates -- LIKE
'abc%', B-tree key comparison on a leading substring -- without
following the chain to reassemble the whole value.  This has no TOAST
analogue: a TOAST pointer carries no user data at all.

2.2 The overflow record header (RecnoOverflowRecordHeader)
----------------------------------------------------------

The off-page chunks are stored as ordinary page items via PageAddItem,
each prefixed by a lightweight header:

    typedef struct RecnoOverflowRecordHeader {
        uint32       or_magic;        /* "ROVC" sentinel */
        uint32       or_data_len;     /* data bytes in this chunk */
        BlockNumber  or_next_block;   /* next chunk page, or Invalid */
        OffsetNumber or_next_offset;  /* next chunk offset */
        uint16       or_flags;        /* reserved */
        /* chunk data follows immediately */
    } RecnoOverflowRecordHeader;

Critically, this header has NO MVCC fields.  An overflow record has no
independent commit timestamp, no delete flag, no visibility of its own.
It inherits the visibility of the parent tuple that points to it.  This
is the same contract TOAST relies on (a TOAST chunk row is only ever
reached through a live heap tuple), but RECNO enforces it structurally
rather than via a second relation's MVCC.

Each chunk holds up to:

    RECNO_OVERFLOW_MAX_CHUNK_SIZE =
        RECNO_MAX_TUPLE_SIZE - RECNO_OVERFLOW_RECORD_OVERHEAD

bytes, where the overhead is MAXALIGN(sizeof(RecnoOverflowRecordHeader)).
A value larger than one chunk is split across a chain of records linked
by (or_next_block, or_next_offset).  RECNO_MAX_OVERFLOW_CHAIN (1024)
bounds chain length defensively in every traversal.

2.3 Host tuple flag
-------------------

The parent tuple sets RECNO_TUPLE_HAS_OVERFLOW (0x0002) in t_flags.
This is the fast-path gate: deform, VACUUM Pass 1, and delete-time
cleanup all short-circuit immediately when the flag is clear, so tuples
with no overflow pay nothing.


3. The Defining Difference From TOAST
=====================================

TOAST stores oversized values in a SEPARATE relation (the toast table,
plus its toast index).  Each chunk is a row in that table, keyed by a
per-value OID (the chunk_id) and a sequence number, with full heap MVCC
on each chunk row, and reached through a btree index lookup.

RECNO overflow stores chunks as records on the SAME data pages that hold
normal tuples.  A page can freely mix live tuples and overflow records;
they are told apart only by the or_magic sentinel and the item length.
There is no overflow relation, no overflow index, no chunk_id, no
per-chunk MVCC, and no index probe to fetch a chunk.  Navigation is a
direct (block, offset) chase identical in spirit to a heap TID walk.

The consequences fan out across every subsystem:

  - Catalog: TOAST adds a pg_class entry (reltoastrelid) and an index
    per toastable table.  RECNO adds nothing -- no extra relation, no
    extra relfilenode, no extra index to keep consistent.

  - Free space: TOAST chunks compete for space only with other TOAST
    chunks in the toast table.  RECNO overflow records draw from the
    SAME FSM as normal tuples (RecnoGetPageWithFreeSpace /
    RecnoRecordFreeSpace), so overflow data and tuple data share and
    balance one free-space pool.

  - Fetch path: TOAST detoast = btree probe on the toast index +
    per-chunk heap fetch, ordered by chunk sequence.  RECNO fetch =
    pointer chase from ov_first_block/offset following or_next_* links
    (RecnoFetchOverflowColumn), no index involved.

  - Locality: a TOAST value's chunks are clustered in the toast table
    by insertion but unrelated to where the parent heap tuple lives.
    RECNO actively co-locates: a chain prefers to place its next record
    on the same page as its previous record (prev_block first), falling
    back to the shared FSM only when that page is full.  The goal is to
    keep a value's chunks physically close so the read-side pin-retaining
    traversal resolves the chain from one page where possible.  (A
    head-page sibling-reuse helper, RecnoFindOverflowPageForReuse, is
    implemented to additionally pack different rows' overflow onto shared
    pages, but is not yet wired into the write path -- see Section 11.)

  - Compression interplay: TOAST does compression and then external
    storage as part of one varlena pipeline (the storage strategies
    plain/extended/external/main).  RECNO runs its own compression pass
    FIRST (recno_tuple.c "Phase 1", RecnoCompressAttribute), then the
    overflow threshold is evaluated against the post-compression size
    ("Phase 1b").  So a value that compresses under the threshold never
    overflows -- exactly the TOAST ordering, but in RECNO's own code.


4. Write Path and WAL Atomicity
===============================

RecnoStoreOverflowColumn() is called during tuple formation, before the
main tuple is placed.  For each chunk it:

  1. Picks a target page: try prev_block (chain locality), else
     RecnoGetPageWithFreeSpace via the shared FSM; extend with P_NEW if
     the FSM has nothing.
  2. Builds the record in palloc'd memory, PageAddItem's it under a
     critical section, and back-patches the previous record's
     or_next_* link to point at it.
  3. Does NOT immediately WAL-log or release the buffer.  Instead it
     appends the (buffer, offset, record_data, len, flag) tuple to a
     caller-supplied RecnoOverflowBuffers collector and keeps the buffer
     pinned AND exclusively locked.

The caller (the INSERT/UPDATE path) then emits ONE WAL record that
registers the main tuple's page together with every collected overflow
buffer, so the entire row -- main tuple plus all its overflow chunks --
becomes durable atomically.  This is the central crash-safety property:
there is never a window where the main tuple is logged but its overflow
chunks are not, which would otherwise produce a tuple pointing at
nonexistent or torn overflow data after recovery.

The WAL record type is xl_recno_overflow_write (recno_xlog.h), carrying
offnum, a flags field, the chunk data_len, and the commit timestamp.
Two flag values:

    RECNO_OVERFLOW_WAL_NEW_RECORD  (0x0000)  full record + data
    RECNO_OVERFLOW_WAL_LINK_UPDATE (0x0001)  header-only link back-patch

The MAX_OVERFLOW_BUFFERS (32) cap bounds how many overflow buffers one
WAL record can carry.  A value large enough to need more chunks than
that, or callers that pass no collector (the legacy entry point), fall
back to immediate per-record WAL logging (RecnoXLogOverflowWrite) inside
the loop -- correct, but not atomic with the parent and therefore
reliant on VACUUM orphan cleanup if a crash intervenes.

FSM robustness: GetPageWithFreeSpace can return a stale block after a
crash/recovery cycle.  The write loop re-checks PageGetFreeSpace after
locking, and on a shortfall it corrects the FSM
(RecnoRecordFreeSpace + FreeSpaceMapVacuumRange) and retries, bounded by
MAX_FSM_RETRIES (100) to convert a corrupt-FSM infinite loop into a
diagnosable error rather than a hang.


5. Read Path
============

RecnoFetchOverflowColumn() reconstructs the full varlena.  It allocates
a result buffer of ov_total_length, then chases the chain from
ov_first_block/offset, memcpy'ing each chunk's data after its header,
following or_next_* until Invalid or until total_length bytes are read.

Spatial-locality optimization in the read path: the buffer is held
PINNED across the chain walk and only re-locked (BUFFER_LOCK_SHARE) per
record.  When consecutive records sit on the same page (the common case,
thanks to the write-side co-location), the pin is reused and no second
ReadBuffer occurs; the pin is dropped only when the chain crosses to a
different page or finishes.  Every chunk is validated (offset in range,
ItemIdIsNormal, or_magic match) and any violation raises
ERRCODE_DATA_CORRUPTED -- overflow corruption is reported, never
silently skipped.

Deform integration: RecnoFormTuple's inverse in recno_tuple.c (and the
slot deform in recno_slot.c) detects an overflow pointer via
RecnoIsOverflowPtr during attribute walk and calls
RecnoFetchOverflowColumn to materialize it.  This is currently EAGER:
the whole value is reassembled in memory on access.  See Section 11.

Lock-ordering note: because overflow records can live on the same page
as the parent tuple, the fetch path must be able to take a share lock on
a buffer the caller may already have pinned.  recno_handler.c sampling
(ANALYZE) and scan paths deliberately drop or avoid holding the parent
buffer's content lock before triggering deform so that
RecnoFetchOverflowColumn can lock the page itself.


6. Delete and Update Paths
==========================

On DELETE or on the old-version replacement during UPDATE, the parent
tuple's overflow chains must be released.  Two helpers cooperate to keep
lock ordering safe:

  - RecnoCollectOverflowPtrs() walks the tuple's attributes and records
    each chain's (first_block, first_offset) into caller arrays WITHOUT
    taking any overflow-page lock.  This lets the caller drop the parent
    buffer's lock first.

  - RecnoDeleteOverflowChain() / RecnoDeleteTupleOverflows() then chase
    each chain taking EXCLUSIVE locks on overflow pages and removing the
    records with RecnoPageIndexTupleDelete (which tolerates LP_UNUSED
    items left by defrag), updating the shared FSM as space is freed.

The separation matters because a chain can start on the parent's own
page; collecting pointers first, then releasing the parent lock, then
deleting, avoids a self-deadlock on that page.

Overflow chain deletion is deliberately NOT WAL-logged per record.  The
rationale (documented at recno_overflow.c) mirrors TOAST: the parent
tuple's modification is WAL-logged, so after recovery the parent either
exists (and its overflow is still referenced) or does not (and its
overflow is orphaned but harmless).  Orphans are reclaimed by VACUUM.
This trades a small amount of post-crash dead space for materially less
WAL on the delete/update hot path.


7. VACUUM Integration
=====================

RecnoVacuumOverflowRecords() (invoked from the RECNO VACUUM path in
recno_operations.c) is a two-pass mark-and-sweep:

  Pass 1 (mark): scan every page; for each LIVE tuple (normal item, not
    RECNO_TUPLE_DELETED) that has RECNO_TUPLE_HAS_OVERFLOW, follow each
    of its chains and insert every (block, offset) it reaches into a
    hash set of referenced overflow locations.  The key is the packed
    uint64 (block << 32 | offset).

  Pass 2 (sweep): scan every page again; for each item that IS an
    overflow record (or_magic match), if its (block, offset) is NOT in
    the referenced set, it is an orphan and is deleted
    (RecnoPageIndexTupleDelete), the page marked dirty and WAL-logged
    via RecnoXLogInitPage, and the FSM updated.  Pass 2 scans each page
    backwards so deletions do not shift offsets still to be examined.

This reclaims overflow records left behind by (a) crashes between parent
deletion and chain deletion, and (b) aborted transactions whose overflow
chunks were written but whose parent tuple is invisible/gone.  It is the
safety net that makes the "don't WAL-log chain deletes" decision sound.

There is also RecnoGetOverflowStats() for monitoring: a full scan
counting overflow records and bytes, used by diagnostics.


8. Anticipated Comparison to TOAST in Practice
==============================================

These are design-level expectations, not yet benchmark-confirmed (see
the caveat at the end of this section).

8.1 Where RECNO overflow should win
-----------------------------------

  - Fewer I/Os per fetch.  TOAST detoast is an index probe plus N chunk
    fetches that may be scattered.  RECNO chases a pointer chain that the
    write path actively co-located, frequently resolving the whole value
    from one or two pages already adjacent to (or identical to) the
    parent's page.  No btree descent at all.

  - No second relation.  No toast-table/toast-index catalog overhead, no
    separate relfilenode to open, fsync, and recover, no toast OID
    allocation contention on high-rate inserts of large values.

  - Prefix predicates for free.  The inline prefix can satisfy
    LIKE 'x%' and leading-key comparisons without any overflow fetch at
    all; TOAST must detoast (at least the first chunk) to do the same.

  - Shared free-space pool.  Overflow space and tuple space draw from
    one FSM, so a workload that deletes large values frees space
    reusable by ordinary tuples on the same pages, reducing the
    bimodal-bloat pattern where a toast table grows independently of its
    parent.

8.2 Where TOAST should win, or where RECNO pays
-----------------------------------------------

  - Very large BLOBs.  TOAST streams and can be read chunk-by-chunk via
    the toast fetch machinery and substring pushdown.  RECNO's fetch is
    currently EAGER (whole value into one palloc'd varlena), so a
    hundreds-of-MB value is a hard problem until the streaming
    enhancement (Section 11, item 1) lands.  TOAST is the mature path
    here.

  - Mixed-page contention.  Because overflow records live on normal data
    pages, large-value writes take exclusive locks on the same pages
    that ordinary DML touches, coupling two workloads that TOAST keeps
    on physically separate relations.  Under heavy concurrent large-value
    churn this can raise buffer-lock contention that TOAST would avoid.

  - Scan skbuffer cost.  Sequential scans must inspect and skip overflow
    records interleaved among tuples (RecnoIsOverflowRecord per item).
    The cost is a magic-word check per item, small but nonzero, and
    absent in a heap whose large values are off in a toast table.

  - Out-of-line sharing/immutability.  TOAST values can be shared by
    reference across row versions in some paths; RECNO overflow is owned
    strictly by one parent tuple version.

8.3 Caveat
----------

No A/B benchmark of RECNO overflow versus heap+TOAST on large-value
workloads has been run as of this writing.  The above is a reasoned
expectation from the data structures and access patterns, and should be
validated with a pgbench-style large-value workload (insert/fetch/update
of multi-KB to multi-MB columns) before any performance claim is made in
a submission.  Do not cite these as measured results.


9. Prior Art and Influences
===========================

The design is a synthesis of three well-understood lineages:

  - Apache Derby long columns / overflow pages.  Derby's raw store
    (org.apache.derby.impl.store.raw.data) stores a row whose columns do
    not fit on a page by writing the oversized columns as "long column"
    portions chained across pages, leaving a pointer (a page+record
    handle) in the main row.  RECNO's per-COLUMN (not per-row) overflow
    decision, the in-tuple pointer-to-first-chunk, and the
    (block, offset) continuation chain follow this model directly.
    Derby likewise keeps overflow portions on ordinary container pages
    rather than a segregated store, which is the same "mix on normal
    pages" choice RECNO makes and the sharpest contrast with TOAST.

  - PostgreSQL TOAST.  The compress-then-externalize ordering, the
    size-threshold trigger keeping the main tuple compact, and the
    "don't WAL-log chunk cleanup; let VACUUM reap orphans" crash-safety
    contract are taken from TOAST's playbook even though the storage
    substrate differs.

  - Classic slotted-page / heap TID chasing.  The continuation pointer
    is a (BlockNumber, OffsetNumber) pair chased exactly like a heap
    item pointer, so RECNO reuses PageAddItem, ItemId inspection,
    PageGetFreeSpace, and the standard FSM rather than inventing a
    parallel allocator.

What RECNO adds on top of these:

  - The inline prefix, for predicate evaluation without fetch.
  - Active write-time co-location (prev_block-first placement) plus the
    matching pin-retaining read-time traversal, to make chain fetches
    cheap.  A head-page sibling-reuse helper exists but is not yet wired
    in (Section 11, item 4).
  - MVCC-less overflow records that inherit parent visibility, avoiding
    a second relation's MVCC and index entirely.


10. Tunables and Limits (quick reference)
=========================================

  recno_overflow_inline_prefix   GUC; default 128 bytes kept inline.
  RECNO_OVERFLOW_THRESHOLD        RECNO_MAX_TUPLE_SIZE / 4; per-column
                                  overflow trigger (post-compression).
  RECNO_OVERFLOW_MAX_CHUNK_SIZE   max data bytes per chunk.
  RECNO_MAX_OVERFLOW_CHAIN        1024; chain-length safety bound.
  RECNO_OVERFLOW_REUSE_SCAN       5; head-page sibling pages scanned for
                                  reuse before extending.
  MAX_OVERFLOW_BUFFERS            32; overflow buffers per atomic WAL
                                  record before falling back to
                                  per-record logging.
  MAX_FSM_RETRIES                 100; stale-FSM retry bound on write.


11. Open Questions / Deferred Work
==================================

  1. Lazy streaming fetch.  RecnoFetchOverflowColumn is eager; a
     streaming varlena wrapper that pages chunks in on demand (releasing
     pins between chunks) is required before RECNO is appropriate for
     very large BLOBs.  This likely means a custom external-pointer type
     integrated with the VARATT_EXTERNAL machinery.

  2. Row-level overflow.  If a row's fixed/inlined fields alone exceed a
     page even after every large column is overflowed, the row itself
     must split.  This needs a new tuple flag
     (RECNO_TUPLE_HAS_ROW_OVERFLOW), a row-continuation pointer, and
     changes to all scan/update/delete paths -- deferred.

  3. Sentinel robustness.  Overflow detection relies on the
     RECNO_OVERFLOW_PTR_MAGIC / RECNO_OVERFLOW_RECORD_MAGIC sentinels
     rather than a tagged varlena bit.  A formal argument (or a tagged
     representation) that no legitimate user varlena can be misread as an
     overflow pointer would harden the deform path.

  4. Cross-row overflow packing.  RecnoFindOverflowPageForReuse is
     implemented (it scans a head page's tuples for sibling overflow
     pages with spare room) but is not yet called by
     RecnoStoreOverflowColumn, which today places via prev_block then
     FSM.  Wiring it in would reduce relation size when many rows on a
     page each carry modest overflow, at the cost of an extra scan on
     the write path.
